- Any other recurring cash payment, including gifts or prizes that are received more than once in a six-month period.

3.3.2 Excluded Income Sources. The following items are not counted as income:

- Federal and state tax refunds;
- Loans, including student loans, personal loans, and proceeds from a reverse mortgage;
- Lump-sum inheritances and life insurance proceeds;
- Capital gains from the sale of a primary residence, if the proceeds are reinvested in another primary residence within twelve months;
- Income from the rental of a room within the applicant's primary residence, up to $300 per month;
- Earnings of a dependent child under age 18 who is enrolled in school, up to $400 per month;
- The value of Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits;
- The value of housing assistance, including Section 8 vouchers and public housing;
- One-time gifts not relied upon for ongoing expenses;
- Disaster assistance payments;
- Income earned by a household member who is a full-time student and whose earnings are used solely for educational expenses.

3.4 Net Income Calculation. For applicants whose gross countable income exceeds the applicable threshold by no more than 15 percent, the eligibility specialist shall calculate net countable income by applying the deductions in this section. If gross countable income exceeds the threshold by more than 15 percent, the application shall be denied without a net income calculation, unless the applicant qualifies for a hardship waiver under Section 6.

3.4.1 Allowable Deductions. The following deductions are applied in the order listed:

